id: treatment
name: Treatment
type: standard
actionCost: 2
kiCost: 0
attacking: false
requiresTarget: true
special: true
usageLimit: 1/round
exploitable: All adjacent Opponents.
clashSkill: medicine
clashDefenderSkills: medicine, craft
treatment: true
source: Core Rule
description: >
  Heal an Ally in your Melee Range for 2d10(bT) plus your Medicine Skill Bonus. If they
  are Poisoned you may halve that to go after the poison - a Clash of Medicine against
  whoever gave it to them, or a Medicine Check where nobody did.
text: >
  Treatment Maneuver [1/Round]: Heal an ally’s wounds.
  –Maneuver Type: Standard Maneuver
  –Action Cost: 2 Actions
  –Minions: N/A
  –KP Cost: N/A
  –Exploitable: All adjacent Opponents.
  –Effects: Target an Ally within your Melee Range. Increase their Life Points by
  2d10(bT) plus your Skill Bonus in Medicine. If an Ally is suffering from the Poisoned
  Combat Condition, you can reduce the amount of Life Points they gain by 1/2 to make a
  Skill Clash (Medicine vs Medicine/Craft: Basic Item) against the Character who gave
  that character the Poisoned Combat Condition. If you win, remove the Combat Condition.
  If they gained the Poisoned Combat Condition other than through the effects of another
  Character, simply make an Medicine Skill Check with the Apprentice Difficulty to remove
  the poison.

---

# --- Healing somebody else ------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The first Maneuver here that puts Life Points on another character. Combat Recovery gives
# them back to whoever used it and does it from its own script, which is the ordinary way -
# but a script writes onto the character running it, so it cannot reach an Ally. The
# Empower Maneuver had the same problem with Ki and is answered the same way: in code.
#
# "2d10(bT)" is two dice per Base Tier of Power - four d10s at Base Tier 2 - which is how
# every other (bT) handful of dice in these rules is read. The Base Tier, so a
# Transformation does not make a doctor better at medicine.
#
# "Plus your Skill Bonus in Medicine", which is the Bonus and not a roll of it. So an
# untrained Medicine still adds something: the Bonus is the Scholarship Score plus 2 a
# Rank, and a character with no Ranks has the Score.
#
# Capped at their maximum, like every other regain here. What actually went on is what the
# card reports, so healing somebody who is nearly full says so rather than claiming a
# number that went nowhere.
#
# --- An Ally, not an Opponent ------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Refused without a target, the way the Empower refuses: "target an Ally" needs one and
# there is nobody to guess at. Who counts as an Ally is the table's - nothing here records
# sides - so anybody may be targeted and the card says who was.
#
# --- The poison ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# "If an Ally is suffering from the Poisoned Combat Condition, you can reduce the amount of
# Life Points they gain by 1/2 to make a Skill Clash..."
#
# A choice, and one that has to be made before the healing is worked out rather than after -
# it is what the healing is halved for. So it is asked when the Maneuver is used, with the
# rest of what can still be taken back.
#
# Offered only when they are actually Poisoned, because the whole clause is conditional on
# it. A card with a button nobody can use is a card that has to be read past.
#
# The halved Life Points are not handed back on a loss. The entry spends them on the
# attempt rather than on the result, and says nothing about getting them back - which is
# the reading every other price in these rules has: what a Maneuver costs is paid for
# using it.
#
# Rounded down, which the entry does not say. Every halving in these rules that does not
# say is read that way - the Thrust's half of your Might, the Ki Wager's half of your
# Capacity - and the ones that round up say so, as the Power Drain and the Feint do.
#
# --- Who gave it to them ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# "Against the Character who gave that character the Poisoned Combat Condition."
#
# Nothing in this system records who inflicted a Condition. Shaken's file has said so since
# it was written, and this is the first Maneuver that needs the answer rather than merely
# describing something that would.
#
# There is one place it could be recorded, and it is real: a Condition given on a clock
# names the character keeping the clock. That is how the Terrify's Shaken works - the
# Condition sits on the target and the entry timing it sits on whoever caused it - so
# `whoInflicted` reads it, and it answers for anything inflicted that way.
#
# Nothing inflicts Poisoned at all yet, and if something did it would probably not put a
# clock on it: Poisoned has no duration of its own and comes off when something takes it
# off. So the answer is asked, with whatever the clock knows offered first. Asking is not a
# fallback here - it is the only thing that can be right about a fact the system does not
# hold, and the table is the one that knows.
#
# --- The two routes ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# "A Skill Clash (Medicine vs Medicine/Craft: Basic Item) against the Character who gave"
# it, or, "if they gained the Poisoned Combat Condition other than through the effects of
# another Character, simply make an Medicine Skill Check with the Apprentice Difficulty".
#
# So: somebody did it, and you race them; or nothing did, and you only have to be good
# enough. Which of the two is the answer to the question above.
#
# The slash on the defender's side is a choice, as every slash here is. "Craft: Basic Item"
# names a specialisation - Craft is an Encompassing Skill and the player writes theirs in -
# and nothing checks what somebody's Craft is about. Said on the card: whether their Craft
# is Basic Items is the table's to judge, and the Skill they roll is Craft either way.
#
# --- The Apprentice Difficulty --------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# There are no Difficulty Categories in this system. Not none for this Maneuver - none at
# all: a Skill Check here is rolled off the sheet or it is one half of a Clash, and nothing
# measures one against a stated number.
#
# So the Check is not rolled by this Maneuver. The card says which Skill and which
# Difficulty, the player rolls Medicine from their own sheet - where the Base Die, the
# critical, the Botch, the Karmic Effects and the refusal for an untrained Required Skill
# all already live - and a button on the card takes the poison off when they made it.
#
# Rolling it here would mean either inventing a number for Apprentice or building a second
# Skill Check path beside the one that works. The first is a rule this file does not have
# and the second is worse than the button.
#
# The Clairvoyance Skill's ARC Tips asked for a Difficulty Category too. That was advice;
# this is a stat line, which makes it the first place the gap is load-bearing.
#
# --- Medicine is Required --------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# No Rank, no roll. So the poison half is refused outright to a character with no Rank in
# Medicine, and said where the choice is offered rather than two steps later - both routes
# are a Medicine roll and neither can be made.
#
# The healing is not refused: it adds the Skill Bonus rather than rolling the Skill, and a
# Bonus is a number an untrained character still has.
#
# Worth saying what this does not fix. A Clash still never asks whether the Skill it names
# can be rolled - the Sense Maneuver's file records that, and it is asked here at the door
# rather than in the Clash, because the door is what this Maneuver owns.
#
# --- What is not built -------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# "Within your Melee Range." Said and not policed, like every range here.
#
# No script. Both halves are about somebody else - the Life Points and the Condition are
# theirs - and a passive writes Slots onto the character holding it.
#
# --- How it is gained ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Medicine, at the 2+ Ranks every Skill grants a Special Maneuver at.
#
# Which was the obvious guess - Medicine is the Skill both halves of the poison clause roll
# and the Skill the healing reads a Bonus off - and the guess was worth nothing until it
# was said: the Dirty Trick is a Clash made with Bluff and Bluff opens the Feint instead.
# Only the Skill's own entry answers it, which is why this line waited for one.
#
# Medicine is Required, so it opens one Rank after the Skill becomes rollable at all.
#
# --- Which makes one of the refusals above unreachable from here -------------------------------
#
# Two Ranks of Medicine is one more than rolling it needs, so nobody who opened this
# Maneuver through the Skill can ever meet the untrained refusal on its poison half.
#
# That does not make it dead. A Special Maneuver has two roads and this is one of them: an
# effect that grants Treatment hands it to whoever it likes, Ranks or none, and that
# character has a Maneuver whose healing works and whose poison half cannot be rolled. It
# is the only way to meet the refusal, and it is the reason it is worth keeping.
#
# Worth noticing what it is not: the refusal is about the character using the Maneuver.
# Whoever they Clash with may still answer with a Medicine or a Craft they have no Rank in,
# because a Clash asks nobody - which the Sense Maneuver's file records and this one cannot
# fix from its own door.
